Title: Blog about the opinion: Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp.

# Crowded Fields and the Sixth DuPont Factor: Federal Circuit Expands the Universe of Third‑Party Uses in Finance

## Why this case matters
Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. matters because it clarifies that, once the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board (TTAB) has found two parties’ services to be highly similar (or even partially legally identical), the Board cannot cabin its sixth DuPont factor analysis to a narrower subset of services. The Federal Circuit reversed the Board for doing exactly that — excluding Aspire‑formative marks used in banking and financing contexts simply because CC Serve’s registration expressly identified “credit card services.” The decision reinforces a broader, more consistent approach to evaluating third‑party uses when assessing a mark’s commercial strength, and it will push litigants and examiners to collect cross‑category marketplace evidence in cases involving adjacent financial services.

## The court’s reasoning in brief
The Government’s central task under Section 2(d) is the DuPont multi‑factor likelihood‑of‑confusion inquiry. The Board had concluded that Apex’s proposed ASPIRE BANK mark and CC Serve’s earlier ASPIRE registration were highly similar in services — the Board found significant overlap between “credit card services” and the banking and financing services Apex identified. Yet, when it came to the sixth DuPont factor (“the number and nature of similar marks in use on similar goods”), the Board restricted its universe to only those third‑party uses expressly tied to credit card services and ignored Aspire uses in other banking and finance contexts.

The Federal Circuit held that was legal error. The court relied on established precedent — including Olde Tyme Foods, Juice Generation, and others — that the sixth factor requires consideration of similar (not necessarily identical) goods or services. When the Board has already found the parties’ services highly similar under DuPont factor two, it cannot then narrow the lens for factor six so tightly as to exclude evidence reasonably related to that same sphere of commerce. Because the Board’s inconsistent scope could materially affect the commercial‑strength analysis and the marks’ overall commercial impression, the court vacated the sixth‑factor finding and also remanded the first‑factor (marks’ similarity/overall impression) analysis for reconsideration in light of a corrected sixth‑factor inquiry.

## Doctrinal basis and precedents
Apex reiterates two stable propositions in trademark law. First, similarity of goods and services is a matter of degree; identity is not required for many DuPont inquiries. Second, evidence of third‑party registrations and marketplace uses is relevant to show a mark’s relative weakness — a crowded field may narrow the senior user’s scope of protection.

The Federal Circuit invoked Olde Tyme Foods and Juice Generation to emphasize that the sixth factor contemplates similar goods, not exact matches, and cited Jack Wolfskin and Juice Generation for the practical proposition that courts sometimes look for a substantial volume of third‑party uses before finding a mark weak. The panel did not disturb those precedents; rather, it applied them to fault the TTAB’s internally inconsistent treatment of similarity across factors. That consistency principle — the Board should use the same factual scope once it has described the relevant marketplace — is the opinion’s practical doctrinal thrust.

## Practical implications for litigants and the PTO
The ruling has immediate, concrete effects for both trademark practitioners and the PTO:

- Collect the broader marketplace record. When litigating (or opposing) marks in the financial services realm, parties should assemble third‑party use and registration evidence across the entire banking and financing ecosystem — not just the exact subcategory named in the registrations. That means capturing Aspire (and Aspire‑formative) uses attached to issuing, processing, servicing, and underwriting financial products, even if the senior registration is narrowly worded as “credit card services.”

- Anticipate a broader relevance standard on remand and in future TTAB disputes. The Board will need to justify any decision to exclude related uses that the Board has already deemed similar in factor two. Parties should craft record narratives showing why a broader set of third‑party uses is probative of consumer familiarity and marketplace crowdedness.

- Consider prosecution strategy. Trademark applicants and practitioners should be mindful that a registration’s identification of services can be a double‑edged sword: a narrowly worded identification does not necessarily limit the Board’s inquiry into relative strength if adjacent services are found similar elsewhere in the record.

## What counts as a “considerable” volume of third‑party use?
The Federal Circuit remands rather than prescribing a bright‑line threshold. Prior decisions have described “considerable” or “voluminous” evidence in concrete terms — for example, the court in Juice Generation referred to roughly two dozen third‑party marks as significant, while Jack Wolfskin highlighted a smaller set of repeated design usages. Those prior numbers serve as guideposts but not fixed rules. Apex highlights two takeaways:

- Quantity matters, but so does quality. A handful of third‑party uses may be persuasive if they are prominent, geographically widespread, or tied to major national providers. Conversely, a larger number of obscure or narrowly local uses may carry less weight.

- Relevance to the defined marketplace is crucial. The Sixth‑factor inquiry asks whether the field is crowded in a way that educates consumers to distinguish similar marks. Evidence that demonstrates consumer exposure across the relevant services — advertising, co‑branding by banks and processors, national registrations, and usages in closely adjacent financial products — will have outsized importance.

Expect appellants to marshal both breadth and depth: enumerating many third‑party marks and documenting their commercial visibility (web presence, advertising, consumer‑facing products) to show that consumers can and do distinguish among similar names.

## Possible downstream effects and litigation strategy
On remand, a broader sixth‑factor inquiry could lead the Board to conclude CC Serve’s ASPIRE mark is relatively weaker and thus entitled to a narrower scope of protection. That in turn could tilt the first‑factor analysis (commercial impression) and the ultimate likelihood‑of‑confusion balance toward Apex. But the decision does not guarantee Apex victory — the second factor (services similarity) was affirmed and remains a strong datum for CC Serve. The outcome will turn on how crowded the record proves the Aspire field to be, and whether the Board concludes that consumers have been conditioned to distinguish among Aspire‑formative marks across banking and financing offerings.

For litigators, the message is straightforward: if you represent a senior user, you cannot rely on a narrow identification of services to immunize your mark from challenges about crowdedness. If you represent an applicant, build a robust record of third‑party registrations and marketplace uses across the entire finance space, and be prepared to show consumer exposure and the commercial prominence of those third‑party users.

## Bottom line
Apex Bank v. CC Serve sharpens a commonsense rule: once the Board identifies the relevant marketplace and finds services similar, it must use that same marketplace lens when evaluating third‑party uses. The decision does not change the governing precedents about similarity or commercial strength, but it requires the TTAB to apply them consistently. For practitioners, the case elevates cross‑category evidence gathering in crowded fields like finance — and signals that the breadth of the marketplace record can be decisive in the commercial‑strength calculus.



# When Credit Cards Are “Banking”: Affirmance on the Second DuPont Factor and the Breadth of Financial Service IDs

## Why this case matters

Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is a useful reminder that, at the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board and on appeal, the textual breadth of an identification of services can be dispositive. The Federal Circuit affirmed the Board’s finding that “credit card services” and “banking and financing services” are highly similar—and in part legally identical—based on dictionary definitions and third‑party registrations. That holding matters for banks, fintechs, and any applicant using broad service descriptions: the way you describe your services on an application can collapse seemingly different businesses into the same commercial universe for likelihood‑of‑confusion purposes, even if one party currently does not offer the other’s services in fact.

## The court’s reasoning, in short

The Board concluded, and the Federal Circuit affirmed, that the parties’ services were highly similar. Apex applied to register ASPIRE BANK for “banking and financing services” (intent‑to‑use), while CC Serve owned an earlier registration for ASPIRE for “credit card services.” The Board relied on dictionary definitions of “banking,” “bank,” and “finance” that encompass extending credit and issuing credit cards, and also on third‑party registrations that list both credit card services and broader banking/finance services under the same marks. Because the identifications overlap, the Board found partial legal identity and weighed the second DuPont factor heavily for likelihood of confusion. The Federal Circuit found substantial evidence supported that conclusion and affirmed.

At the same time, the court vacated and remanded the Board’s treatment of the first (similarity of marks/ commercial impression) and sixth (number and nature of similar marks in use) DuPont factors. The court reasoned that the Board’s narrow construction of the relevant third‑party marks under the sixth factor (limiting consideration only to marks expressly tied to “credit card services”) was inconsistent with its broader finding under the second factor that the services are highly similar; the same scope of services should inform related DuPont inquiries. Because reconsideration of third‑party uses could affect the perceived commercial strength of CC Serve’s ASPIRE mark, the court also remanded the first factor.

## What the opinion teaches about identification breadth

There are three lessons for practitioners and registrants:

First, identification language can determine whether services are treated as similar or even legally identical. The Board and the Federal Circuit relied on the plain meanings of “banking” and “finance” to encompass credit card issuance and account servicing. That is classic TTAB/Trademark Office reasoning: registration rights are defined by the operative identification submitted to USPTO, not by the registrant’s current business model. In practice, this means an applicant that uses broad IDs—“banking services,” “financial services,” “consumer lending services”—may find those IDs sweep in adjacent offerings such as credit cards, payment processing, or account servicing when a conflict arises.

Second, actual non‑competition today does not control. Apex does not currently issue credit cards, but its application for “banking and financing services” was vulnerable because the ID encompasses credit card issuance and related services. The inquiry under DuPont focuses on the nature of the goods/services as identified, and on the possibility of overlap in channels, consumers, or source confusion, not solely on present marketplace overlap. That doctrinal point is old hat in trademark law, but Apex underscores its bite in the fintech context, where partnerships and program relationships make service boundaries porous.

Third, third‑party registrations and definitions matter. The Board relied on third‑party registrations showing that marks have been registered for credit card and banking services under the same or similar marks. Such evidence helps establish that consumers may deem the services emanating from a single source under one mark. Conversely, ample third‑party use of a formative term on similar services can narrow a mark’s scope of protection (the sixth DuPont factor), but the Federal Circuit here required the Board to use a consistent scope when assessing that evidence.

## Implications for fintech-bank partnerships and applicants

Fintechs that partner with banks to issue co‑branded or bank‑issued credit cards, issuing banks that outsource program management, and applicants planning to enter adjacent financial markets should take away a cautionary practical point: marks used for one slice of financial services are not insulated from claims by owners of marks used in related financial offerings simply because different entities operate different pieces of the stack.

A fintech that offers card program management but does not issue cards might nonetheless be vulnerable if its mark overlaps with an earlier mark used on card issuance, because consumer confusion can arise from the source‑perception of the financial product (the card and account) rather than the internal business arrangements. Likewise, a bank that later decides to offer cards under a brand might find prior users in the card‑services space blocking registration.

For applicants, narrow and particularized identifications can be defensive. Where possible, tailor identifications to the specific services actually offered (e.g., “program management services for bank‑issued credit card accounts” rather than the broad “banking and financing services”), or prepare to argue why specific services fall outside the ordinary meaning of a broad term. But be mindful: narrowing during prosecution can trigger other tradeoffs, such as limitations on potential future expansions or arguments about intent‑to‑use scope.

## Doctrinal and downstream effects

Doctrinally, the Federal Circuit’s decision reaffirms two well‑established principles: (1) the legal significance of the identification language in an application/registration, and (2) the importance of consistent scope when applying multiple DuPont factors. Practically, the opinion signals to the TTAB and practitioners that when the Board finds services similar or partially identical under DuPont factor two, it must carry that scope into analyses under factor six (third‑party usages) and factor one (commercial impression), lest its analysis be internally inconsistent.

The remand on factors one and six is meaningful. If a fuller accounting of third‑party uses shows that “aspire” is widespread across the broader constellation of banking and finance services, CC Serve’s claim to exclusive protection may weaken and the first‑factor comparison may tilt differently. Conversely, if third‑party uses are limited when measured across the appropriate broader scope, CC Serve stands to benefit from normal protection for an inherently distinctive mark. The remand keeps the ultimate ambiguity alive and highlights that broad IDs both help and hurt: they can pull in a challenger’s services, but they also expose the mark to dilution arguments across a wide field.

For examining attorneys and applicants alike, the case reinforces careful ID drafting and thoughtful use of third‑party evidence. Examiners and the Board should avoid artificially constraining the universe of comparable services or third‑party marks in a way that contradicts their own prior findings about service similarity.

## Bottom line and practical tips

Apex Bank affirms the old maxim that words matter in trademark practice. Broad identifications like “banking and financing services” commonly encompass credit card issuance and account servicing. Applicants should (1) draft IDs to reflect actual services and foreseeable brand plans, (2) be prepared to justify narrow IDs if seeking to avoid conflicts, and (3) gather third‑party evidence that is measured to the full scope of service similarity the Board uses. Banks and fintechs engaged in program partnerships should also recognize that the source perception of financial products (cards, accounts) can create trademark exposure even when backend roles are split.

The Federal Circuit’s remand on commercial strength and third‑party use means this is not the last word on ASPIRE’s scope of protection—only a firm reminder that the identification box you check on your application can be as consequential as the business plan you execute.



# Strength Begets Impression: Why the First DuPont Factor Falls with the Sixth

## Why Apex Bank v. CC Serve matters

The Federal Circuit’s decision in Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is a crisp illustration of an important, sometimes underappreciated point in Lanham Act doctrine: the evidentiary showing of third‑party use (DuPont factor six) does not live in isolation — it directly shapes the commercial impression (DuPont factor one) and therefore the overall likelihood‑of‑confusion inquiry. By affirming the Board’s finding that Apex’s and CC Serve’s services are highly similar but vacating the Board’s treatment of factor six and, consequentially, factor one, the court made plain that a re‑calibration of a mark’s commercial strength can change how similarity of the marks is assessed. That ripple effect matters for litigants and practitioners: building or rebutting a case of crowding in a mark space can narrow or expand the scope of protection and materially alter the outcome of an opposition or infringement claim.

## The court’s reasoning in brief

The Board had sustained CC Serve’s opposition to Apex Bank’s application to register ASPIRE BANK for banking and financing services, finding a likelihood of confusion in light of CC Serve’s earlier ASPIRE registration for credit card services. The Federal Circuit affirmed the Board’s pivotal factual finding that the parties’ services are highly similar — even “partially legally identical” — which supports treating third‑party marks for both credit‑card and broader banking/finance services as relevant.

Where the Board went astray, the court held, was in its limited view of the universe of third‑party uses under the sixth DuPont factor. Although the Board determined the parties’ services to be highly similar when analyzing factor two, it restricted factor six to third‑party uses tied narrowly to “credit card services,” excluding marks used in connection with other banking and financial services. The Federal Circuit rejected that inconsistent narrowing. Because factor six is meant to account for similar marks used on similar goods or services — not strictly identical ones — the Board should have retained the scope it adopted in factor two when evaluating third‑party evidence. The court therefore vacated the Board’s factor‑six finding and remanded.

Critically, the court also vacated the Board’s analysis of the first DuPont factor — similarity of the marks in appearance, sound, connotation, and commercial impression — explaining that the strength (or weakness) shown by a proper factor‑six analysis can alter the “commercial impression” that the Board draws. In short: if a mark is shown to be weak or crowded, its owner is entitled to a narrower scope of protection and the comparison of marks must proceed with that narrowed lens. Because the Board’s factor‑six error could change its evaluation of ASPIRE’s commercial strength, the factor‑one assessment had to be revisited too.

## Why commercial strength and commercial impression are linked

The decision rests on a straightforward doctrinal premise with real analytic bite: commercial strength is not an isolated attribute that only affects damages or entitles a markholder to bolder relief; it is a contextual fact that informs how confusing two marks will appear to the ordinary consumer. When many competitors use ASPIRE‑formative marks across closely related financial services, consumers are presumably conditioned to distinguish among slightly different uses of “aspire.” That conditioning reduces the likelihood that consumers will attribute a new ASPIRE‑derived branding to the single source who owns a prior registration. Consequently, evidence of crowding under factor six diminishes the “commercial impression” that factor one seeks to capture.

The Federal Circuit’s remedial step — vacating factor one rather than treating it as untouched by a revised strength finding — is doctrinally sound. The Board’s commercial‑impression determination cannot be insulated from evidence that undermines a mark’s distinctiveness or suggests a crowded marketplace. Practically, that means the trademark comparison is iterative: findings under one DuPont factor can and should influence how other factors are weighed.

## Doctrinal and practical implications

This opinion underscores several rules of thumb for practitioners:

- Consistency of scope across factors matters. If the Board finds services to be “highly similar,” it should use that same breadth when identifying third‑party uses that are “similar” for factor six. Narrowing scope midstream invites reversal.

- Factor six is potentially dispositive on scope. A robust showing of third‑party use across the relevant range of services can shrink the protective bubble around a mark, changing the baseline against which the competing mark is judged under factor one and beyond.

- Commercial impression is pliable. It’s not a fixed snapshot of the mark in isolation; it’s a judgment shaped by marketplace realities. Evidence that a mark is common in a field may convert ostensibly similar marks into lesser risks of consumer confusion.

Downstream, remands like this will produce Board decisions where once‑routine comparisons become more nuanced: boards will need to assemble a record of third‑party use that reflects the full range of “similar” services they have already found similar under factor two. Petitioners opposing a mark — or applicants defending registration — should anticipate more careful, record-anchored analyses of market crowding and its effects on the marks’ commercial impressions.

## Practical guidance: building (or defeating) a third‑party‑use record

For parties looking to influence the remand, here are practical steps grounded in the opinion’s logic:

- When you assert similarity of services, be prepared to accept a broader universe for third‑party evidence. If you argue that banking and credit card services are highly similar, gather third‑party examples not only of credit‑card marks but of marks used for general banking, lending, payments, and related financial services. The Federal Circuit called for precisely that consistency.

- Don’t rely solely on registrations. The Board (and courts) care about actual marketplace use as well as registrations. Collect evidence of active use: screenshots of live websites, advertisements, marketing materials, customer-facing accounts, and screenshots showing co‑brand or co‑service offerings (e.g., banks issuing credit cards under partner marks).

- Quantify and contextualize. The Board distinguished “nine” Aspire uses in a field from the kinds of voluminous third‑party use that demonstrate weakness (the court cited precedents turning on larger numbers). Help the factfinder understand whether alleged uses are isolated, regional, or national; whether they represent actual consumer exposure; and whether they are contemporaneous.

- Show consumer education, if available. If there is evidence consumers routinely distinguish among ASPIRE‑formative marks (advertising that clarifies different providers, marketplace comparisons, or consumer surveys), that supports a finding of narrow protection. Conversely, absence of such education strengthens the owner’s position.

- Tailor arguments to consumer class. The Board erred by narrowing the public to credit‑card consumers while treating services more broadly elsewhere. Be explicit about who the “ordinary consumer” is and how their purchasing context affects the weight of third‑party evidence.

## How a recalibrated strength finding could shift the confusion analysis

A remand that finds the ASPIRE family is widely used across banking and payment services could have several ripple effects. First, it could narrow the scope of ASPIRE’s protection, making ASPIRE BANK less likely to be held confusingly similar because consumers are more accustomed to distinguishing among ASPIRE‑formative brands. Second, a weaker mark diminishes the force of other DuPont factors — not by eliminating them, but by changing the baseline for what constitutes a confusing overlap in commercial impression. Third, the relative weakness could free an applicant to register more nuanced variations (design or stylized marks) that previously looked perilously close.

Conversely, if the Board’s expanded review still shows only modest third‑party use concentrated in niches or at a small scale, CC Serve may retain a robust scope of protection and the original likelihood‑of‑confusion finding could survive. The Federal Circuit’s remand is thus not pro‑applicant in the abstract; it simply requires a legally consistent, record‑based treatment of how crowded the ASPIRE space actually is.

## Conclusion

Apex Bank v. CC Serve is a timely reminder that trademark law demands coherence across its multiple, interacting inquiries. Evidence that a mark is crowded in the marketplace does more than affect abstract questions of dilution or scope — it reshapes the commercial impression against which similarity is judged. For litigants, that means building the right record matters: a well‑documented showing of third‑party use can narrow protection and tilt the likelihood‑of‑confusion balance, while poor indexing of the competitive landscape risks letting a single board finding dominate an otherwise multifaceted inquiry. The Federal Circuit’s ruling brings that interaction into sharper relief and gives practitioners a clear map for how to marshal — or rebut — evidence of crowding on remand.



## Policing TTAB Consistency Across DuPont Factors: A Substantial‑Evidence Case That Turned on Legal Framing

Why this matters — Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is another reminder that while the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board’s factual findings receive substantial‑evidence review, the Federal Circuit will step in where the Board shifts its legal framing from one DuPont factor to another. The panel here affirmed the Board’s key factual finding — that CC Serve’s credit‑card services and Apex’s banking/finance services are highly similar — but vacated the Board’s treatment of the sixth factor and, consequentially, its analysis of the first factor. The error: the Board narrowed the universe of third‑party “Aspire” uses it would consider under DuPont 6 to a slice of the services even though it had already concluded the parties’ services overlapped broadly under DuPont 2. That mismatch turned what might have been a run‑of‑the‑mill likelihood‑of‑confusion opinion into a Federal Circuit remand.

What the court did and why

The Board sustained CC Serve’s opposition to Apex’s ASPIRE BANK application under Section 2(d), largely on the premise that the services were highly similar and the marks close enough to create a likelihood of confusion. On appeal the Federal Circuit applied the familiar standard: legal conclusions de novo, factual findings for substantial evidence. It affirmed the Board’s DuPont 2 holding — the services are highly similar (in part legally identical) — because the Board’s parsing of the parties’ identifications and supporting evidence was supported by record citations.

The Court’s reversal targeted a legal inconsistency. Having found the services highly similar for DuPont 2, the Board then confined its DuPont 6 inquiry to third‑party uses tied narrowly to “credit card services,” excluding Aspire uses in other banking/finance contexts. The Federal Circuit flagged that approach as erroneous: DuPont 6 asks about “the number and nature of similar marks in use on similar goods,” not only identical goods, and the Board’s earlier DuPont 2 finding defined the relevant scope of similarity. Once the Board decided the services were highly similar, it could not impose a different, narrower definition of similarity for DuPont 6 without legal error. Because DuPont 6 bears on a mark’s commercial strength and thus on the overall impression relevant to DuPont 1, the Court vacated both factors and remanded for the Board to reassess the universe of third‑party Aspire uses and then revisit the marks’ overall commercial impression.

Context and doctrinal line

Apex Bank fits into a steady line of Federal Circuit decisions policing how the TTAB treats third‑party evidence and how the Board defines the “relevant public” or scope of goods/services when applying DuPont. The court has repeatedly emphasized that the Board must be coherent in its legal framing: it cannot, for example, find goods to be related for purposes of confusion yet ignore that relatedness when considering the breadth of third‑party use. Decisions such as Juice Generation and Jack Wolfskin have long guided that DuPont 6 is about relative weakness and the practical marketplace reality that consumers might distinguish crowded marks; Apex reinforces that the universe of potentially crowding uses must be set consistently with the Board’s own DuPont 2 findings.

Two discrete themes are worth underscoring. First, DuPont factors are interlocking; factual findings under one factor often bear on the legal import of evidence under another. A Board determination that services are highly similar should generally expand — not contract — the set of third‑party uses that are “similar” enough to inform DuPont 6. Second, the Board’s purposive inquiry into commercial strength cannot be artificially narrowed by technical pinning of a third‑party mark to a single inventory description when the Board itself has said the marketplace overlap is broader.

Practical takeaways for litigants and the Board

For litigants: preserve your legal objections to the Board’s framing of the relevant market and the scope of “similarity.” Apex shows the Federal Circuit will correct legal inconsistencies even where factual findings stand. If the Board finds goods or services related under DuPont 2, press to ensure that any third‑party evidence you submit is considered in that same, consistent scope under DuPont 6. If opposing, be ready to show why third‑party uses should be limited (e.g., materially different channels, customer classes), not merely to argue numbers.

For the TTAB (and practitioners drafting decisions): Apex is a drafting reminder. When the Board makes a substantive finding about the nature and scope of goods/services, it should explicitly carry that scope into related-factor analyses or explain why a narrower lens is appropriate. If the Board intends to confine DuPont 6 to more specific subcategories, it should ground that limitation in record facts (channel distinctions, separate classes of consumers, service differences) rather than in a conclusory exclusion. Doing so reduces reversal risk and gives appellate courts a clear chain from facts to legal conclusions.

Downstream effects

Apex is unlikely to change the basic playbook: parties will still marshal third‑party registrations and marketplace uses to argue for relative weakness or strength. But the decision reinforces an allocation of tasks between factfinder and court: the Board gets broad leeway on mixed factual assessments, but its legal framing — especially when it shifts during an analysis of interconnected factors — is fair game for correction. Expect litigants to cite Apex when urging broader consideration of third‑party evidence once relatedness is established, and expect the Board to be more explicit about the universe of marks it treats as “similar” so its DuPont narrative is coherent across factors.

Conclusion

Apex Bank v. CC Serve exemplifies a modest but important supervisory role the Federal Circuit plays over TTAB methodology. The core mistake was not necessarily the outcome the Board reached, but the Board’s inconsistent legal framing across DuPont factors. By insisting that the scope of similarity found under DuPont 2 carry through to DuPont 6 (unless justified by record distinctions), the court protects the coherence of the likelihood‑of‑confusion analysis and limits opportunistic narrowing that can mask a mark’s true commercial strength. Practitioners and the Board would do well to take that coherence seriously in both record development and opinion writing.



# Legacy Identifications and Deleted Terms: Interpreting “Credit Card Services” in 2025

## Why this case matters

Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. (Fed. Cir. 2025) is a useful waypoint for litigants and practitioners wrestling with “legacy” trademark identifications—terms that were valid when a registration issued but have since been altered or removed from the Trademark ID Manual. The Trademark Trial and Appeal Board in Apex Bank confronted just that problem: CC Serve’s registration identified “credit card services,” a term later deleted from the ID Manual. The Board nonetheless construed the legacy identification using dictionary definitions and marketplace context, and the Federal Circuit, while rejecting part of the Board’s reasoning on other DuPont factors, accepted the Board’s construction of the services for the second DuPont factor (similarity of goods/services). The decision underscores how courts and the Board will treat aging or deleted identifications and signals practical consequences for both registrants trying to preserve older filings and applicants seeking clarity about the scope of prior rights.

## How the Board and Federal Circuit approached a deleted ID

At issue was whether CC Serve’s plain-text registration for ASPIRE (issued in 1998) should be read to cover a range of activities embodied by the phrase “credit card services.” Because “credit card services” had been deleted from the ID Manual subsequent to CC Serve’s registration, the Board did not rely on a current ID Manual definition. Instead it turned to dictionary definitions and market-context evidence to spell out what “credit card services” encompassed—issuing cards, processing transactions, account management, credit counseling, fraud alerts, and the like. The Board then compared that construction to Apex’s proposed banking and financing services and concluded the services were highly similar, even partially legally identical.

On appeal Apex argued that CC Serve’s services were not the same as Apex’s (particularly because CC Serve partners with banks to service card programs rather than itself “being a bank”). The Federal Circuit affirmed the Board’s finding under the second DuPont factor, stating that the Board had carefully tied the services it construed to the written identification and that substantial evidence supported the conclusion of high similarity. The court therefore accepted the Board’s methodology in defining the legacy phrasing for purposes of the services-similarity analysis.

The court did, however, vacate the Board’s analysis under DuPont factors six (third‑party use) and one (mark similarity), not because of the definition itself but because the Board narrowed the universe of third‑party “Aspire” uses to only those expressly labeled “credit card services.” Having already found the parties’ services highly similar or even partially legally identical, the court held that the Board should have applied the same (broader) conception of similarity when assessing third‑party marks and the marks’ overall commercial impression.

## What this means doctrinally

Apex Bank reinforces several legal touchstones and clarifies their application in the context of legacy IDs:

- Legacy identifications can and will be construed by the Board and courts using extrinsic sources when the ID Manual no longer defines the term. The Board is not confined to the current ID Manual if the registration predates a change.
  
- The governing question remains what the identification, viewed in context, reasonably encompasses. The Board’s use of dictionaries and marketplace evidence to flesh out the meaning is consistent with the general principle that an identification defines the legal scope of the registration.

- Substantial-evidence review governs. Because the court affirmed the Board’s factual finding that the services are highly similar, parties will face a high bar to overturn such constructions absent record‑deficient reasoning.

- Consistency across DuPont factors matters. Once a particular scope of goods/services is adopted in one part of the analysis (here, factor two), that scope should inform consideration of other factors—especially those that turn on the field of use (e.g., third‑party marks under factor six or commercial impression under factor one). The Federal Circuit corrected the Board for applying inconsistent scopes.

## Practical implications and strategies

For registrants with older registrations
- Legacy registrations remain valuable. Even if an ID term was later deleted from the Manual, a registration’s plain-language identification can be construed to include a range of services so long as the Board or a court finds that construction supported by evidence.
- Preserve evidentiary support for marketplace meaning. Registrants should develop and maintain contemporaneous evidence that what the written identification covered at issuance corresponds to industry practices—advertising, catalogs, agreements with partners, contemporaneous dictionaries, and trade publications can all help cement a broad reading later.

For applicants and challengers
- Don’t assume deletion from the ID Manual narrows preexisting registrations. An applicant can expect the Board to consider dictionary and marketplace context when evaluating legacy IDs. Attackers should be prepared to rebut proposed constructions with evidence showing a narrower historical understanding or differences in function between the old term and the applicant’s services.
- Attack the factual foundation. When facing a legacy ID construed broadly on dictionaries and market usage, challenge whether the Board’s particular factual findings have substantial evidentiary support. Absent weak factual findings, reversal is unlikely.

For both sides during prosecution and litigation
- Use extrinsic evidence proactively. The Apex Bank opinion validates using dictionaries, third‑party registrations, industry materials, and testimony to define the operative scope of an identification. Parties should marshal such evidence early.
- Be consistent in scope across arguments. If you succeed (or fail) in establishing a particular scope for factor two, expect that same scope to be relevant to factors that ask whether the field is crowded or how the marks are perceived commercially. Courts will not tolerate “moving the goalposts” by expanding or narrowing the relevant universe between factors.

## Final thoughts

Apex Bank offers a practical lesson: deletions from the ID Manual change the guidance the PTO gives going forward, but they do not erase the legal life of earlier identifications. The Board and courts will look beyond the Manual to dictionaries and marketplace realities to understand what a legacy identification meant and whether that meaning overlaps with an applicant’s goods or services. That approach is defensible on the merits—language evolves, and the Trademark Act ties rights to the registration’s identification, not to a shifting online manual—but it also injects uncertainty. Practitioners should therefore treat identifications as living documents: preserve usage evidence, use extrinsic materials strategically, and be prepared to insist on consistent treatment of scope across the DuPont inquiry.



# Branding Lessons from ASPIRE BANK: Picking Names in an Aspirational Finance Landscape

Why this case matters
Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is a reminder that one of the riskiest naming strategies in financial services is to pick an “aspirational” word and assume the field is empty. The Federal Circuit’s decision—affirming the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board (TTAB) that the parties’ services are highly similar, but vacating the Board’s treatment of third‑party use and the marks’ overall commercial impression—underscores three practical points for brand owners and clearance counsel: (1) aspirational and generic‑feeling words (like ASPIRE) attract preexisting use across related financial services; (2) broadly worded identifications of services will pull otherwise distinct offerings into a single relatedness bucket; and (3) robust, carefully framed evidence of third‑party use matters a great deal to the scope of protection and the likely outcome of disputes.

What the court did (brief walkthrough)
CC Serve owns an older federal registration for ASPIRE for credit card services. Apex filed intent‑to‑use applications to register ASPIRE BANK for “banking and financing services” (and a design). The TTAB sustained CC Serve’s opposition under Section 2(d), finding a likelihood of confusion. On appeal the Federal Circuit applied the DuPont framework. Two holdings are most important for brand clearance and litigation strategy.

First, the court affirmed the Board’s finding on the second DuPont factor: Apex’s “banking and financing services” are highly similar to CC Serve’s credit card services. The court accepted the Board’s construction of the term “credit card services” to include issuing and managing cards, processing transactions, authorization/monitoring and related services. Because the services overlap conceptually and operationally, substantial evidence supported the Board’s conclusion that the services were highly similar—so similarity of services weighed strongly in favor of a likelihood of confusion.

Second, the court vacated the Board’s treatment of the sixth DuPont factor (third‑party use of similar marks) and, because that could materially affect the marks’ commercial impression, remanded the first DuPont factor as well. The Board had confined its sixth‑factor analysis to third‑party marks used specifically for “credit card services,” excluding uses of ASPIRE in other areas of banking and finance. The Federal Circuit explained that once the Board found the parties’ services highly similar (indeed, partially legally identical), it was inconsistent to narrow the universe of third‑party marks for the sixth factor to a tighter category. The sixth factor permits consideration of marks on “similar” goods or services, not only identical ones; the Board should have considered the broader set of ASPIRE uses across banking and financing services. Consequently, the Board’s assessment of the marks’ commercial strength and the overall likelihood‑of‑confusion calculus must be revisited.

Practical takeaways for brand owners and clearance counsel

1) Aspirational words are a crowded lane in finance
“ASPIRE” is literally designed to sell a positive feeling. That very attractiveness makes it a common choice across banks, card issuers, fintechs, and ancillary financial services. When a word is conceptually appealing to many market actors, expect multiple third‑party uses. Those uses can either weaken an earlier mark’s scope (if numerous and public) or, if not fully explored, be overlooked in a way that later surprises applicants. In practice: treat aspirational terms as presumptively higher‑risk and be ready to expend clearance effort to map the competitive landscape across all related financial offerings—not just the narrow subcategory you intend to enter.

2) Broad identifications of services materially increase relatedness risk
Apex’s applications identified “banking and financing services.” Broad ID language traditionally favors opposers because it reaches many activities that may overlap conceptually with other players’ services. The court’s acceptance of the Board’s definition of “credit card services” as encompassing many common banking functions shows how an applicant’s broad description can collapse distinctions that, in market terms, feel meaningful (e.g., branch banking vs. card program management). Practical response: narrow descriptions during prosecution when possible (subject to commercial realities) and draft use specimens and specimen descriptions carefully to emphasize distinct channels, functions, or customer bases.

3) Don’t over‑rely on “we don’t issue cards” arguments
Apex argued that CC Serve simply partners with banks to offer branded credit cards and therefore is not a bank. The court gave that argument short shrift: function and consumer perception matter more than corporate structure. If consumers reasonably perceive services as related or supplied through partnerships, courts and the TTAB will treat them as related for DuPont purposes. For applicants, that means evidence about how consumers experience the service (who they think provides it, how it’s marketed, where it’s found) can be decisive.

4) Be methodical and expansive in collecting third‑party use evidence
The Federal Circuit vacated the Board’s sixth‑factor finding because the Board artificially restricted the universe of relevant third‑party ASPIRE uses. That reversal underlines how critical it is to assemble comprehensive third‑party evidence: registrations, live uses, screenshots, advertising, and press coverage across the whole banking/financial ecosystem—not just the immediate subcategory. If multiple third‑party users of an aspirational term in related services can be shown, that evidence can support a narrower scope of protection for an earlier mark and decrease the likelihood of confusion.

Doctrinal implications and likely downstream effects

- Consistency across DuPont factors matters. The opinion stresses internal consistency: if the Board finds high relatedness for purposes of factor two, it should maintain that scope in evaluating third‑party use under factor six. This reinforces a unitary, context‑sensitive approach to DuPont and limits opportunistic narrowing of the universe of comparable marks. Practitioners should be prepared to argue both breadth and narrowness of the relevant market consistently across DuPont factors, depending on which side they represent.

- Commercial impression is fluid and fact‑sensitive. Because the court remanded the first factor, it acknowledged that conclusions about the marks’ overall strength and impression may change once the full set of third‑party uses is considered. That signals that rulings on commercial strength are not purely legal abstractions but can pivot with more complete factual records. In practice, both opposers and applicants should plan for litigation or TTAB proceedings where the record is expanded—expect remands and reweighing.

- Clearance search strategies should go beyond exact matches. The Federal Circuit’s reasoning encourages broader clearance searches capturing formative uses (ASPIRE‑plus), uses in related financial submarkets, and non‑identical but conceptually similar branding. Clearance reports that focus narrowly on identical marks in identical goods risk undercutting strategic decisions.

Effects on settlement leverage and digital banking launches
The remand changes the tactical calculus for both sides. CC Serve won the key early battle—services are highly similar—so it retains leverage. But the vacatur of the sixth and first factors gives Apex a second bite: a successful showing of widespread ASPIRE uses across the broader banking/finance space could undercut CC Serve’s exclusivity and reduce the scope of protection, thereby improving Apex’s bargaining position.

For banks and fintechs planning online launches, the case suggests several steps to preserve agility and reduce litigation risk. First, when selecting an online brand, run expansive searches and consider pluralities of use (variations, compound marks, domain names, social handles). Second, if you proceed with an aspirational name, build a record of distinctiveness early: unique stylization, consistent channels, disclaimers, and customer surveys can help. Third, consider narrowing the goods/services descriptions where commercial operations permit—especially in applications intentionally targeting online or narrowly defined product families.

Conclusion
Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. offers a pragmatic lesson: in finance, attractive single‑word names invite competition and legal friction. The Federal Circuit affirms that relatedness of services can sweep in businesses that are merely partners rather than direct competitors, and it insists that the TTAB evaluate third‑party use at a scope consistent with its relatedness findings. For brand owners, that means think broadly in clearance work, be strategic in describing goods and services, and collect exhaustive third‑party use evidence to shape the scope of protection you can realistically obtain.